+          <p><code>VisionCaptureToolLoop.swift</code> does not validate or require target fields for an ordinary chat turn. Only after Gemma requests high-level navigation does the host validate and inject the exact bundle identifier and Simulator UDID. The host alone retains one unambiguous returned session pair, refreshes the cache before each app-owned mutation, and holds the newest observation grant or exact action/revalidation capability. Gemma never sees or proposes any of those values. VisionCapture can return a new flow when an existing flow no longer resolves under its explicit-only policy, so a successful <code>launch app</code>, <code>inspect cache</code>, or <code>describe screen</code> response may roll the retained flow forward after target validation. A changed session from an error or mutation response remains terminal. Multiple conflicting returned pairs remain terminal, pairs split across separate objects are not joined, and a response without a clear pair does not create one. When the host&rsquo;s own read-only <code>inspect cache</code> returns <code>CACHE_LIVE_VALIDATION_FAILED</code>, the adapter treats it as pre-dispatch only when dispatch evidence is absent or explicitly false, never true or conflicting. It discards the cache handles and sends VisionCapture&rsquo;s existing <code>describe system alert</code> read. One unambiguous present alert becomes sanitized title-and-button facts. Gemma still chooses an ordinary high-level tap whose role is <code>system_alert_button</code>; the adapter then sends VisionCapture&rsquo;s existing <code>press system alert button</code> once with the private exact label and digest. Neither alert request carries a flow session. If the alert read proves no native alert is present, the host sends one plain read-only <code>describe screen</code> without a cache grant or capability and returns only sanitized facts. It does not repeat <code>inspect cache</code> or retry the discarded action. For stale cached-action recovery, VisionCapture&rsquo;s structured <code>payload.view.signature_fine</code> remains private and exists only to prove exact changed versus unchanged state. The adapter accepts only one unambiguous fine value and does not weaken that comparison. Separately, bounded no-progress memory uses the public coarse signature plus a digest of sorted, de-numbered sanitized accessibility facts. This ignores session and fine-signature churn but changes when meaningful visible controls, roles, labels, or structural selection change. A ready or revalidation cache block can publish actions without a fine signature. When that happens after a mutation cleared the previous identity, the adapter conditionally discards those handles, makes one plain target-bound <code>describe screen</code>, records both private identities, then calls <code>inspect cache</code> once for fresh usable handles. VisionCapture&rsquo;s public contract does not accept its output-only <code>screen_capability</code> as a read input, so the adapter never relabels or sends it. No extra describe is added when the current fine identity already exists, and the cold observation-grant route remains unchanged. Gemma receives the comparison result and an optional single confirming high-level action, never either signature, the digest, or a discarded capability.</p>
